From 1ad02d6b1ef14a625cfd660d5a69ee372c8ffce2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work10_kasan_plan <adbtrr_work10_kasan_plan@local>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:23:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mm/kasan: add a self test that proves the instrumentation is
 live

A board with no memory errors and a board where the instrumentation was
never emitted both boot silently, so a clean boot says nothing on its own
about whether KASan works. This is the other half of that check: one
deliberate memory error, on request, with a report as the pass condition.

The faulting access is made by the kernel against a block from the kernel
heap, and the app only reaches it over a new PR_KASAN_SELFTEST prctl, the
same bridge PR_MEM_LEAK_CHECKER uses. In a protected build user space
allocates from a heap that is never registered with KASan and is not
instrumented, so a test written entirely in user space would report
nothing however broken the memory manager was and would read as a pass.

The kernel side is in kernel/debug/ and not under mm/. Everything under
mm/ is compiled with -fno-sanitize=kernel-address so the sanitizer does
not instrument itself, which also means an access made from there is
never checked.

Six cases: an in bounds access that must stay silent, overflow by one
byte for both a write and a read, underflow into the node header,
use after free, and a word write that begins in the last accessible
machine word and ends in the first poisoned one. The last is the case a
shadow test that looks only at the size of an access misses, and the one
this port fixed on the way in from the reference implementation.

The in bounds case is the negative control and should be run first. If it
reports, the allocator hook is unpoisoning less than it hands out and no
other result is meaningful.

Two details worth stating because they are easy to get wrong:

The test computes the accessible extent as
MM_ALIGN_UP(request + SIZEOF_MM_ALLOCNODE) - SIZEOF_MM_ALLOCNODE rather
than using the request. mm_malloc() hands back a whole chunk and the hook
unpoisons all of it past the node header, so an overflow is reported past
the end of the chunk and not past the end of the request. Writing at
request[size] would often land on a byte the allocator legitimately made
available, nothing would be reported, and the result would read as a
failure of the instrumentation rather than of the test. Using the same
expression as the allocator keeps this correct if either constant changes
with the configuration.

Every access goes through a volatile, which stops the optimiser dropping
a store nobody reads, stops constant propagation reaching the offset, and
on the use after free case makes the pointer opaque enough that
-Wuse-after-free stays quiet without disabling it for the file.

Gated on CONFIG_MM_KASAN_SELFTEST, default n, which builds both the
kernel side and the app. The enum is declared unconditionally so user
space can name the cases without knowing how the kernel was built.

Verified by replaying the allocator's poison and unpoison sequence
against the real shadow map with the target's constants
(SIZEOF_MM_ALLOCNODE 16, 16 byte alignment, 4 byte shadow scale): all
five fault cases land on a poisoned granule, the in bounds case does not,
and two extra controls confirm the straddling write is not caught by
accident. 9 of 9. New sources produce no diagnostics under -Wall where an
existing file in the same directory produces one, and the added prctl
case adds none.

The app is in apps/system/ next to mem_leak_checker rather than
apps/examples/ as the plan document said. It is a debug tool paired with
a kernel feature, not an example.
